cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "d1bench: which Cloudflare D1 write strategy wins, and which limit stops you first"
message: "If you use this software or its measurements, please cite it using these metadata."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Vincent
    family-names: Gonzalez
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0005-3640-014X"
    email: vincegonzalez@me.com
repository-code: "https://github.com/vince-gonzalez/d1bench"
license: MIT
abstract: >-
  Cloudflare documents D1's limits one at a time. It does not document how they
  interact, and they interact in a way that changes which bulk-insert strategy
  is correct. This harness deploys inside a Worker, because two of the limits
  under test only exist inside a Worker invocation, and sweeps three write
  strategies across column count and row size against a live database.

  Five measured results. A single db.batch() call accepted 150,000 statements,
  150 times the documented 1000-query per-invocation cap, with row counts
  reconciled exactly against statements sent. The query-count limit is
  therefore not charged per statement inside a batch. The limit that does bind
  is the 30-second duration cap applied to the entire batch call, exactly as
  the documentation's own footnote states: 150,000 statements succeeded in
  25.8 seconds and 175,000 were refused. The practical ceiling is therefore a
  time budget rather than a statement count, moving with row width, and a
  number copied from these tables into code handling wider rows will be
  wrong. The bound-parameter ceiling is
  exactly floor(100/C), confirmed by climbing until D1 answers "too many SQL
  variables" at every column count, which means that at 51 or more columns
  chunked bound parameters has silently become one statement per row. Literal
  SQL is 2.7 times faster at 64 columns. On the row-size axis it reverses and
  ends in a wall: past roughly 131 KB of row a single literal tuple exceeds the
  100,000-byte statement limit and literal SQL is refused outright rather than
  merely slower. The result those four are consequences of is that the two
  strategies are capped by orthogonal limits, bound parameters binding on
  column count and invariant to row size, literal binding on row size and
  invariant to column count.

  A methodological note is included because it changes how any Workers
  benchmark should be read. In Workers, Date.now() advances only on I/O and is
  frozen across pure computation, so a clock inside the Worker cannot observe
  the CPU spent building a statement, and literal SQL is the strategy doing
  that work. Every run is therefore timed from inside and outside. Measured
  outside overhead was 84 to 98 ms and identical between strategies at every
  column count, establishing that the invisible CPU did not produce the
  reported gap.

  Limitations are stated rather than implied: one region, one database, one
  account, one day, no indexes, and no sweep of escaping cost. The ratios and
  the orthogonality are the claims; the absolute timings are evidence for them.

keywords:
  - Cloudflare D1
  - Cloudflare Workers
  - SQLite
  - database benchmark
  - bulk insert
  - bound parameters
  - serverless
  - reproducible measurement
